WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.532
Python is frequently used to process files and structured data, so validation must go beyond web requests.

00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:11.807
Filenames should be treated as data, not trusted paths.

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:16.489
File paths should be resolved and checked against intended directories.

00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:20.763
File size limits should be enforced before expensive processing.

00:00:20.763 --> 00:00:27.888
Extensions can be useful hints, but they should not be treated as proof of file content.

00:00:27.888 --> 00:00:32.570
JSON parsing confirms syntax, but schema validation confirms expected structure.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:40.509
A valid JSON object may still be missing required fields, contain unexpected nested data, or use wrong types.

00:00:40.509 --> 00:00:46.412
YAML requires extra caution because unsafe loaders can process data in dangerous ways.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:53.130
Prefer safe loading behavior and explicit schemas when YAML is accepted from users or integrations.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.405
XML risks should be understood at a high level.

00:00:57.405 --> 00:01:03.308
XML parsers can behave differently depending on features, external entities, and resource handling.

00:01:03.308 --> 00:01:08.804
Developers should use safe parser configurations and libraries appropriate for untrusted XML.

00:01:08.804 --> 00:01:19.186
CSV files look simple, but they can contain unexpected delimiters, formulas, encodings, huge rows, missing columns, and data that violates business rules after import.

00:01:19.186 --> 00:01:22.239
Structured data validation should be layered.

00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:28.957
First parse safely, then normalize where appropriate, then validate structure, types, sizes, and business rules.

00:01:28.957 --> 00:01:37.710
Avoid assuming that a file is safe because the extension looks right or because it came from a familiar integration.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:43.614
The validation contract should describe the data the application actually intends to process.
